  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Startling Decline (2012) in New England Journal of Medicine. 2 citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Startling Decline (2012) in New England Journal of Medicine is authored by Anand Vaidya, Brigid M. Dolan and Brian L. Edlow. Topics: Medicine, Personality, Cognition, Cognitive decline and Cognitive psychology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">2 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include BMJ and New England Journal of Medicine.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2012 New England Journal of Medicine case report has an early but focused scholarly footprint: it has been cited 2 times, with downstream reuse recorded in 3 places. The citing record is concentrated in clinical and biomedical venues, including BMJ and the New England Journal of Medicine, suggesting that the piece was taken up primarily within professional medical discussion.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Startling Decline is a clinically framed case report about an older man with a rapid change in cognition and personality, published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2012. The article\u2019s impact record is modest in volume but clear in character. OpenAlex shows 2 citations, both appearing in the same publication year, which indicates an early and immediate scholarly response rather than a long citation tail.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape points to disciplinary relevance across dementia-focused clinical scholarship and related biomedical discussion. One citing work appeared in BMJ on symptomatic treatment of dementia, and another in the New England Journal of Medicine. That pattern suggests the article has been used as a clinical reference point rather than as a broadly cited foundational study.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citations, the record shows 3 documented reuse events, including an Europe PMC article mention. These signals indicate that the article continued to circulate in biomedical indexing and article-level reuse pathways, even though the overall volume of downstream engagement remains limited in the available data.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other forms of public discourse are not reported in the record, so the most defensible interpretation is that this was a specialized, clinically visible article with concentrated uptake in medicine and neuroscience-adjacent contexts. The evidence available here supports recognition of targeted professional use, but not broad public attention.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}